WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.800
With a running time of about two-and-a-half
hours, Captain America: Civil War is one stuffed

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.160
movie. It features about four action-heavy
set-pieces, a dozen superheroes beating each

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.940
other up, political machinations, and even
a couple of bromances thrown in for good measure.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.790
As a result, some scenes just had to hit the
cutting room floor. As ever, beware: spoilers

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:21.630
ahead.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:22.290
Meet Ant-Man

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:26.930
When we meet Ant-Man before the big super-throwdown
at the airport in Germany, he more or less

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.570
just jumps out of a van and shakes hands with
Captain America. But as it turns out, the

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:36.930
tiny hero's introduction in the script was
originally, well, bigger. Get it, cause he grows? He grows big.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.620
According to a Slashfilm interview with Civil
War's screenwriters Christopher Markus and

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.210
Stephen McFeely, the script originally called
for a scene with Lang and the gang in his

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.249
home of San Francisco. But it was deleted
because it didn't get Ant-Man's tone quite

00:00:48.249 --> 00:00:49.609
right. Markus explained:

00:00:49.609 --> 00:00:53.710
"We were probably wrong. Because of the nature
of when we start making the movie versus when

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.159
it comes out, as we’re doing it for Infinity
War, we wrote a lot, if not all, of Scott

00:00:58.159 --> 00:01:01.699
Lang’s stuff before we ever saw Ant-Man.
You just have to roll with that because we

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:02.620
need to film."

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.870
Where's Wasp?

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.640
Speaking of Ant-Man, fans were excited by
that movie's post-credits scene that alluded

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.130
to the impending debut of the hero's crime-fighting
partner, the Wasp. I mean maybe she'd appear with

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:16.979
Ant-Man in Civil War, right? That's apparently
something the filmmakers actually wanted to

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:17.409
happen.

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:21.880
But Marvel Studios head Kevin Feige explained
it was more important to preserve her superheroic

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:23.630
journey for the Ant-Man sequel.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:24.700
Talk it out

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.689
After the Winter Soldier is captured in Germany,
the heroes gather and discuss what's going

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:32.789
on. These scenes continue to inform who's
on which side of the movie's conflict, and

00:01:32.789 --> 00:01:37.719
why. But Civil War's directors, Joe and Anthony
Russo, decided not all conversations are created

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:38.180
equally.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.770
Joe Russo explained to ComicBook.com that
a Black Widow and Black Panther chat was cut

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:43.740
to keep things rolling:

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.520
"There was a scene between Natasha and T’Challa
at the German Ops Center, halfway through

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.560
the movie when he was sitting in that room.
Cap and Falcon had been isolated in the other

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.909
conference room. We didn’t feel like it
was moving the storytelling forward and they

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:59.490
had already had a scene so it felt repetitive
so it was something we excised from the movie."

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:00.700
Cap dies?

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.520
The end of Captain America: Civil War shows
Cap ultimately winning his brawl with Iron

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.859
Man, and living to fight another day. But
at the end of the comic book version of Civil

00:02:07.859 --> 00:02:09.660
War, Cap meets his maker.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.690
As it happens, a ScreenRant interview with
screenwriters McFeely and Markus reveals that they

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.580
never even considered offing Captain America.
Killing off Cap, said McFeely, would've undercut

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:22.090
the ambiguity they were aiming for with the
issues presented by the Sokovia Accords:

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:26.530
"It also makes him too definitely a martyr
and makes it feel like he was right. And we

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.860
wanted neither side to be right or wrong.
We wanted to end it kind of queasy. Both opinions

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.739
are still standing at the end of the movie."

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:36.140
And considering how great the final film turned
out, it's pretty hard to argue with their

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:36.720
decision.

